[{:seon.task/name "a-resource-name-stays-in-its-root"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by jolt/similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding (2026-10-23): in a worktree nested under the main tree (next/.worktrees/NAME), `seon.config-test/an-unknown-key-refuses-boot-naming-it` and `an-invalid-value-refuses-boot-naming-each-key` read the MAIN tree's resources/seon/config/default.edn, so a branch that adds config keys is red there and one that removes keys could be falsely green. Cause: Jolt's `resource-candidate` (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/java/io.ss, called from `resolve-resource`) builds root + \"/\" + name with no check that the normalized path stays under root, and aero's adaptive resolver tries `clojure.java.io/resource` before the including file's directory, so test/seon/config/unknown-key.edn's #include \"../../../resources/seon/config/default.edn\" climbs from the worktree's src/ root out to next/resources/. The JVM's resource lookup answers nil for a name that leaves its root. Reproduction, in a nested worktree: HOME=$PWD/target/home JOLT_PWD=$PWD target/jolt/jolt -A:test -e '(println (str (clojure.java.io/resource \"../../../resources/seon/config/default.edn\")))' prints a file: URL into the main tree; the JVM prints nil. Correct, in our Jolt fork on a jolt/<topic> branch: `resource-candidate` answers no candidate for a name whose normalized path leaves its root. Notes: side-session-notes config-include-leak.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
